{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Deploy the supplied stdio handler on node1 through inetd or xinetd, listening on TCP port 9097 with a separate handler process per connection.","status":"satisfied","summary":"OpenBSD inetd was installed and enabled, with a persistent nowait service on TCP 9097 running the supplied handler as nobody. Twelve simultaneously held connections produced twelve distinct child handler processes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-527eaf244f044438","cmd-e8eaca7a19264018","cmd-d5f4d8bde0ff4354","cmd-8cd0a4d27fe04ccf","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"From the legacy client on node2, PING followed by a newline must return PONG, while other lines return ERROR.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct node2-to-node1 tests returned PONG for PING and ERROR for HELLO, an empty line, OTHER, SOMETHING, and INVALID requests, including after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f06d2a37aa7043d3","cmd-9f5be7659c844d9d","cmd-8de570f14df64741","cmd-e14a716694ec46bc"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Multiple sequential and concurrent clients must complete correctly without leaving handler processes behind.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Sequential requests completed correctly. A controlled 12-connection test showed one handler per live connection, all clients then completed with expected responses, and the handler count returned to zero. A final 20-client concurrent test also produced 20 correct responses and zero remaining handlers.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f06d2a37aa7043d3","cmd-1c6bd80d47684c07","cmd-d5f4d8bde0ff4354","cmd-b403ab98e4874547","cmd-37fba8cc84de4d94","cmd-e14a716694ec46bc","cmd-ac4998cdd92e4d70"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the protocol notes in /srv/legacy2/inetd-request-activation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final protocol.txt content and SHA-256 hash match the prepared baseline; the supplied handler hash also remained unchanged.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-5b5178efa4ad462b","cmd-7c286350d9654c09","cmd-8cd0a4d27fe04ccf","cmd-ac4998cdd92e4d70"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on Ubuntu 24.04.3 LTS with kernel 6.8.0-71-generic. The inetd package came from the Ubuntu Noble repository, and package consistency remained healthy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ecacbfc8b8f642ee","cmd-20ddd3d1f3584986","cmd-527eaf244f044438","cmd-e14a716694ec46bc","cmd-ac4998cdd92e4d70","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"The deployed service must remain operational as a persistent installation after node1 restarts.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before restart, node1 had an enabled, active, responsive service with no lingering handler. The restart was acknowledged, the boot ID changed, and afterward inetd was enabled, active, listening, responsive, retained its configuration and data, and again left zero handlers. The fixed lifecycle observations independently confirm the boot transition.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7c286350d9654c09","cmd-25223f3211d8486a","cmd-b58896dcf52b4fc2","cmd-f82d2b07c5bf4a2c","cmd-8cd0a4d27fe04ccf","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Installing inetd and its resolved dependencies (cmd-527eaf244f044438) and adding/restarting the service configuration (cmd-e8eaca7a19264018) directly established the requested service. Temporary node2 files and processes created for the bounded concurrency test (cmd-6cd31615ea884b2b, cmd-b403ab98e4874547) supported final behavioral evidence and were absent from the final lifecycle snapshot after restart. The node restart actions (cmd-25223f3211d8486a, cmd-1e728a2b1fb64c40) were required by the provider-wide evidence protocol. Final observations show healthy package state, no failed services, only the required new port-9097 listener, and no abandoned test files.","evidence_ids":["cmd-527eaf244f044438","cmd-e8eaca7a19264018","cmd-6cd31615ea884b2b","cmd-b403ab98e4874547","cmd-25223f3211d8486a","cmd-1e728a2b1fb64c40","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The service correctly handles sequential and concurrent node2 clients, creates one process per connection, leaves no handlers behind, preserves the supplied files, retains the OS and kernel, and recovers after restart. The mandatory restart protocol is complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and demonstrated recovered functionality, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots.","limitations":[]}
